nts i think  the threshold for me is if somebody gets  hospitalized it's a breakthrough  infection they've already been  vaccinated vaccinated but we're not  quite there yet and these vaccines do  very well against the variants  especially the uk variant when it comes  to what matters serious illness  hospitalization and death and i think we  have to keep returning to that that's  what we're trying to do  tame this virus defang it make it unable  to threaten hospital capacity again and  our vaccines are all very good against  all the variants when it comes to that  that end point as more and more people  get vaccinated i'm seeing it i'm seeing  this sort of euphoria  of people in the sense we're going to  get back to normal and sooner than we  may have expected  but you said in salon this week that you  believe there has been an  over emphasis on what you cannot do  once you're vaccinated can you elaborate  well when you see people talking about  the vaccine sometimes they'll say well  you have to go and basically nothing  changes and it's not true  things do change when you get vaccinated  you're basically protected from  symptomatic disease  you're very less likely to spread this  virus to somebody else  and if you're doing things for example  if you're going out and  maybe you're somebody that's an early  adopter of going to indoor dining you're  at very low risk of getting serious  infection  and when you're with another person  that's vaccinated you can basically do  anything  with that other vaccinated person  because of the because you're on the  same timeline because you're both  protected  and if you're seeing somebody that's  unvaccinated the risk to you is very  very low so i do think that this does  change  change your life and i think that people  are really often kind of underselling  the vaccine  saying well this doesn't do this this  doesn't do this and  i think what we're looking at are really  tremendous vaccines that are going to be  the path to normalcy  that the people should be optimistic  about them and if we don't tell people  that  that this vaccine is going to benefit  their life if it's going to improve  their life  they're not going to get the vaccine and  i think we have to really think about  how we're talking about this vaccine in  the media  quickly you you mentioned that that this  notion of covet  zero is a fantasy why  you have to remember that coronaviruses  are part of the viruses that cause the  common cold they cause about 25  of our common colds this virus spreads  like that it spreads efficiently through  the respiratory route it's established  itself in the human population  we will never get it down to zero the  goal as i said before  is to tame it so that it doesn't cause  severe illness does it so that it can't  hospitalize people or threaten hospital  capacity and we'll get there with the  vaccine  but this isn't going to be an irratible  disease or it's it's not going to be  eliminated we'll still have covet cases  five years from now but they're never  going to be able to threaten us the way  they can now  once we get people vaccinated we  certainly hope so  dr amish adulterer thank you
 

